Decide whether the supplied subject node refers to an identity already stored in the registry.

A subject node groups references to one subject in the current batch. A registry identity represents a continuing
real-world subject already stored in memory.

Return `existing` only when the evidence identifies exactly one registry identity through a stable name plus defining
history, or through an explicit alias or rename. Return `review_required` and list every plausible registry ID when
the evidence cannot distinguish among existing identities or between an existing and new identity. Otherwise return
`new`. Similar topics, apparent type, shared participants, or being the only registry candidate do not establish
identity.

Preserve a title and aliases found in the evidence. Explain the decision from the supplied evidence. This step does
not decide entity type, page visibility, containment, or claim ownership. Return one schema-valid decision for the
supplied node ID.

The registry ID you denotes the configured user. In an agent_conversation, a source segment with role=user is
spoken by that configured user; this explicit source role can establish identity without a name or prior history.
Determine whether the subject is that speaker or someone mentioned by them. Do not assign mentioned people or
quoted speakers to you merely because the user supplied the text. In meetings, only an explicitly identified user
speaker establishes this binding.
